You are debugging a production issue where a high-throughput transactional service experiences unbounded heap usage and intermittent cache misses. The team implemented a custom key class for a `ConcurrentHashMap` used as an in-memory cache:

java
public class TransactionKey {
    private String transactionId;
    private List<String> metadataTags;

    public TransactionKey(String transactionId, List<String> metadataTags) {
        this.transactionId = transactionId;
        this.metadataTags = metadataTags;
    }

    public void addTag(String tag) {
        this.metadataTags.add(tag);
    }

    @Override
    public int hashCode() {
        return Objects.hash(transactionId, metadataTags);
    }

    @Override
    public boolean equals(Object o) {
        if (this == o) return true;
        if (!(o instanceof TransactionKey)) return false;
        TransactionKey that = (TransactionKey) o;
        return Objects.equals(transactionId, that.transactionId) &&
               Objects.equals(metadataTags, that.metadataTags);
    }
}


During transaction processing, worker threads retrieve existing keys and execute `key.addTag("PROCESSED")` to update execution state.

You are debugging a production issue where an in-memory cache implemented by extending `LinkedHashMap` occasionally throws `NullPointerException` or hangs threads during concurrent read traffic. The class is implemented as follows:

java
public class BoundedCache<K, V> extends LinkedHashMap<K, V> {
    private final int maxCapacity;

    public BoundedCache(int maxCapacity) {
        super(16, 0.75f, true);
        this.maxCapacity = maxCapacity;
    }

    @Override
    protected boolean removeEldestEntry(Map.Entry<K, V> eldest) {
        return size() > maxCapacity;
    }
}

You are debugging a production issue where a Spring `@Service` uses asynchronous worker threads to populate a custom thread-safe collection buffer before writing to a database within a transaction scope:

java
@Service
public class OrderBatchProcessor {

    @Autowired
    private OrderRepository orderRepository;

    @Transactional
    public void processBatchAsync(List<OrderRequest> requests) {
        List<Order> synchronizedBuffer = Collections.synchronizedList(new ArrayList<>());

        List<CompletableFuture<Void>> futures = requests.stream()
            .map(req -> CompletableFuture.runAsync(() -> {
                Order order = buildOrder(req);
                synchronizedBuffer.add(order);
            }))
            .collect(Collectors.toList());

        CompletableFuture.allOf(futures.toArray(new CompletableFuture[0])).join();
        orderRepository.saveAll(synchronizedBuffer);
    }
}


During worker thread execution, database locks and unexpected partial commits occur when secondary transactional services are invoked inside `buildOrder()`.
